    <h1>Proposal: Inclusive Atlantis</h1>
    <p>Conceived by the Disability Inclusion Research Initiative (DIRI), "Inclusive Atlantis" is a comprehensive, community-based research project designed to develop and test a scalable, inclusive framework for improving the quality of life for people with disabilities across diverse urban and rural settings.</p>
    <p>Named after the mythological lost city of Atlantis—symbolizing a vision of a society where all individuals can thrive—this project aims to bridge the gap between policy aspirations and real-world implementation by engaging stakeholders at every level: individuals with disabilities, caregivers, healthcare providers, educators, policymakers, and community leaders.</p>
    <p>Guided by the principles of universal design, inclusive education, accessible infrastructure, and social equity, Inclusive Atlantis will focus on three core pillars:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Accessible Urban Environments: Designing public spaces, transportation systems, and housing that are physically, technologically, and socially inclusive—ensuring that all individuals can navigate and participate safely and independently.</li>
        <li>Empowering Education & Workforce Integration: Developing inclusive curricula, vocational training programs, and workplace accommodations that support the full participation of people with disabilities in education and employment.</li>
        <li>Community Support & Mental Well-being: Building peer support networks, mental health services, and digital literacy programs tailored to the needs of diverse disability communities, with a focus on reducing isolation and promoting resilience.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Through a mixed-methods approach combining surveys, focus groups, participatory action research, and pilot implementation in three pilot cities (one in the U.S., one in the UK, and one in Australia), Inclusive Atlantis will:</p>
